    "question": {
      ":question/id": "49097f81-bd1a-500c-9aed-944233ba4b3a",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3z &=-3 \\\\ y+4z &=4 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nR_{2}:=R_{2}-4R_{3},\\qquad M\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n$$\nR_{1}:=R_{1}+3R_{3},\\qquad M\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ y &=-4 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=3$, $y=-4$, and $z=2$. Or, in vector form, $\\langle 3,-4,2\\rangle$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
